    <%# == Where this run's wall clock went, by directory
        The rung directly above the panel above, off the same rows of the same run, and NOT
        derivable from it. That ranking is by FILE and ten rows long: a directory holding forty
        files at two seconds each is eighty seconds of the run with not one of its files near the
        head of it. Concentration re-concentrates at every rung — which is why each rung is summed
        rather than read off the one below — and this panel asks the question the reader usually
        arrives with: which AREA of this suite carries the time.

        Grouped on the immediate parent of the INCLUDING file (`spec_file_path`), so a shared
        example group's time lands on the area that ran it rather than on `spec/support` — the same
        rule the two panels above obey, one rung up. Every row sits at the same depth as its own
        file, so the areas partition the run rather than nesting inside one another and the totals
        sum to it. Depth selection and drill-down trees are a different question and not this one.

        NOT the shard rollup on the Overview panel. `TestRun#shard_durations` splits the same run by
        CI partition and its own comment is explicit that a shard is not a code area: RSpec/Knapsack
        partitions are arbitrary with respect to directory structure, so "which partition ran long"
        and "which area costs" are two questions and this is the second.

        ONE run, like the panels above, and for the same reason: `example_id` is positional and not
        stable across refactors, so anything spanning runs would be comparing rows not known to be
        the same tests.

        Same visibility rule as the panels above — rendered when the run WROTE example rows, and a
        run with rows but no timings renders and says so. Outside the `manage_keys` gate: read-only
        suite telemetry, nothing credential and nothing actionable. %>
    <% if @spec_directory_durations&.recorded? %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Heaviest spec directories", id: "spec-directory-durations",
                                        class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
        <% if @spec_directory_durations.any_timed? %>
          <%# What the totals COVER, before the totals themselves — the argument the panel above
              makes, where it costs more. `SUM` skips a missing timing silently, and an area is a
              bigger population than a file, so an area whose examples were half untimed prints a
              total covering half of it that is indistinguishable, as a number, from a complete one.
              The per-area coverage column is the answer, and this paragraph is what tells a reader
              the column is a denominator rather than decoration.

              Every figure is counted off THIS RUN'S ROWS, never off `TestRun#total_specs_count` on
              the Overview panel above — that count is re-derived by SUM over shard reports and the
              two can legitimately differ. `SpecDirectoryDurations` carries the argument and holds
              the rows and their coverage together, so this sentence cannot end up describing a
              different row set from the one below it. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="spec-directory-durations-basis">
            <%# The list is CAPPED, so its own length says nothing about how much of the run it
                covers — the same lie by omission the coverage column refuses one grain down, which
                is why the total is stated whether or not anything was cut. It costs no extra
                query: the count rides back on the same grouped aggregate. %>
            <% if @spec_directory_durations.truncated? %>
              The <%= number_with_delimiter(@spec_directory_durations.rows.size) %> heaviest of the
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@spec_directory_durations.directory_count) %> directories
              the run named above recorded, heaviest first
            <% else %>
              All <%= number_with_delimiter(@spec_directory_durations.directory_count) %>
              <%= "directory".pluralize(@spec_directory_durations.directory_count) %> the run named
              above recorded, heaviest first
            <% end %>
            — every example's duration summed onto the directory holding the file that ran it, so a
            shared example group counts against the area including it rather than against the one
            defining it. Each area is the immediate parent of its own files, so these totals sit
            side by side rather than inside one another.
            <% if @spec_directory_durations.complete? %>
              Every example in every directory listed reported a duration, so each total covers the
              whole of its area.
            <% else %>
              The "Examples timed" column is what each total was summed over: an area whose examples
              did not all report a duration has a total covering only the ones that did, and an area
              that reported none has no total to state rather than a zero. An example that never ran
              has no duration to report, so a missing timing is a faithful record rather than a gap.
            <% end %>
            Counted off the rows this run wrote here, which is a different figure from the suite
            size on the Overview panel above and may legitimately differ from it. It is also not the
            shard split on that panel: a CI partition is not a code area.
            <%# What the THIRD column was counted over, on the same terms as the second — and the
                sentence that stops the new figure being read as a verdict.

                `COUNT(DISTINCT name)` skips a missing description exactly as `SUM` skips a missing
                timing, and inverted it costs more than anything else on this page: an area whose
                producer sent no descriptions would print zero distinct behaviors against its whole
                example count, which is the strongest over-coverage reading available here and is
                made of silence. So the column is a fraction over the NAMED rows, the excluded rows
                are printed beside it, and an area with none says so.

                Every sentence here is a claim about the areas LISTED, never about the run — the
                list is capped at ten and the sentence one paragraph up says so out loud ("the 10
                heaviest of the 13 directories"). `#any_named?` and `#fully_named?` fold over those
                ten rows, so a run whose only described area sits below the cap would make a
                run-scoped sentence here false while both predicates are correct. The predicates are
                list-scoped by construction and the prose has to be too, which is the rule
                `SpecDirectoryDurations` states about itself: a caption is a claim ABOUT the list. %>
            <% if @spec_directory_durations.any_named? %>
              The "Distinct descriptions" column is how many distinct descriptions each area's
              examples carry, counted over the examples that reported one.
              <% if @spec_directory_durations.fully_named? %>
                Every example in every directory listed reported a description, so each count covers
                the whole of its area.
              <% else %>
                Examples that reported no description are counted separately beside it rather than
                folded in, and an area that reported none has no descriptions to count rather than
                zero distinct behaviors.
              <% end %>
              Several examples sharing a description is equally a suite testing one behavior many
              ways and an ordinary table-driven loop or shared example group, so this is a ratio to
              review rather than a judgement about the area.
            <% else %>
              The "Distinct descriptions" column has nothing to state about the directories listed
              here: not one of their examples reported a description, so there is nothing to count
              distinct rather than nothing distinct to count.
            <% end %>
          </p>
          <%# "Distinct descriptions" LAST, in the order the area-grain reading is said in: this
              area carries N examples ("Examples timed"), costs this much wall clock ("Total
              duration"), for this many distinct behaviors. %>
          <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Directory", "Examples timed", "Total duration",
                                                      "Distinct descriptions"],
                                            describedby: "spec-directory-durations-basis") do %>
            <% @spec_directory_durations.rows.each do |directory| %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">
                  <%# The way INTO the area, and the rung that was missing. Every ranking on this
                      page is a capped ten, and this one is the LEAST escapable of them: the by-file
                      panel above is also ten rows long, so an area that is heavy because it holds
                      forty ordinary files has not one of its files in that list — its rows were
                      reachable from nowhere at all. The path was already being rendered, so the
                      drill-down costs no query to offer, only the one it takes when a reader asks.

                      `?spec_directory=`, following the `?spec_file=` precedent directly above and
                      the `?branch=` one before it rather than a nested route: a directory path
                      contains `/` and makes a poor route segment, and the panel and its drill-down
                      read one run on one page.

                      Through `drill_down_path`: every OTHER ask rides through and only
                      `spec_directory` is set here. That includes an OPEN FILE, which this link
                      dropped from the day it was written — `?spec_file=` had shipped the day
                      before — while "Close directory" below carried the file back the other way,
                      so opening an area destroyed what closing one preserved. The rule is stated
                      once in the helper now rather than re-decided in forty hand-written
                      arguments, which is how that asymmetry survived a later edit to this very
                      list that ADDED a different ask without noticing the missing one.

                      `aria-current` on the open area, because this is a list of choices with one of
                      them taken and the panel below is a long way down the page. %>
                  <%= link_to directory.path,
                              drill_down_path(@repository, spec_directory: directory.path,
                                              anchor: "spec-directory-files"),
                              class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                     "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                              aria: { current: directory.path == @spec_directory_request ? "true" : nil } %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%# Always a fraction, never a bare count: the denominator is the point of the
                      column, and a complete area has to be visibly complete rather than merely
                      unremarked. %>
                  <%= directory.coverage_label %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content tabular-nums">
                  <%# Through `SpecObservation.humanized_duration`, the same seam the per-example
                      and per-file columns above render through — so no two grains on this page can
                      disagree about how a duration is spelled, and an area that measured nothing
                      says "not reported" rather than wearing the spelling of a zero. %>
                  <%= directory.duration_label %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%# The third figure of the area-grain reading, and the one that must never be a
                      bare number. A fraction over the rows the count could SEE, because
                      `COUNT(DISTINCT name)` skips the rows it could not, and "no descriptions"
                      rather than "0" for an area none of whose examples reported one — a zero
                      there is this surface inventing the most redundant area on the page out of a
                      producer's silence.

                      The excluded rows are printed beside the fraction wherever the fraction is
                      printed, the way every coverage figure on this page carries its own
                      denominator rather than deferring to a caption a reader may not be looking
                      at.

                      SPELLED LIKE THE FRACTION IT ANNOTATES — no `number_with_delimiter`, and that
                      is a decision rather than an omission. The two halves of this cell are one
                      sentence, and delimiting only the second reads "40 of 20000 (1,234 unnamed)"
                      at the design point: one cell disagreeing with itself about what a number
                      looks like. The fraction's spelling is `Row#distinct_description_label`'s,
                      which is `#coverage_label`'s, which is the spelling of the "Examples timed"
                      cell one column over and of the same fraction at every other grain on this
                      page — so the alternative is not "delimit this span" but "re-spell every
                      coverage fraction the panels share", which is a change to all of them and is
                      deliberately not smuggled in behind a new column. Delimiters on this page mark
                      CAPTION PROSE, where a figure stands alone in a sentence; every other
                      `number_with_delimiter` in this file is in a basis line or a summary string. %>
                  <%= directory.distinct_description_label %>
                  <% if directory.named? && directory.excluded_unnamed_rows? %>
                    <span class="text-app-muted">
                      (<%= directory.unnamed_count %> unnamed)
                    </span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
          <%# THE WIDENING ASK — the directories panel's own copy of the control the by-file panel
              above renders, to the SAME shared ceiling and through the SAME `drill_down_path`
              carry. The `?limit=` ask names a magnitude, not a panel, so both rollups widen on
              one URL and each panel offers its own way to see it: a reader widening from here
              gets the same widened by-file list above, and the captions on both panels restate
              their own population at the new size off the same `COUNT(*) OVER ()` figure.

              Same rules as the sibling's copy, and for the same reasons stated there: rendered
              only when `truncated?` leaves headroom below the ceiling; and beside it the way
              back, an explicit `limit: nil` override, because a widening in the URL rides every
              link on the page and a bare link would keep it. %>
          <% if @spec_directory_durations.truncated? &&
                @spec_directory_durations.rows.size < RequestedLimitParam::MAX_LIMIT %>
            <p class="text-xs mt-2">
              <%= link_to "Show up to #{number_with_delimiter(RequestedLimitParam::MAX_LIMIT)} heaviest areas",
                          drill_down_path(@repository, limit: RequestedLimitParam::MAX_LIMIT,
                                          anchor: "spec-directory-durations"),
                          class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                 "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content" %>
            </p>
          <% end %>
          <% if @limit_request %>
            <p class="text-xs mt-2">
              <%= link_to "Back to the #{SpecObservation::HEAVIEST_DIRECTORIES_LIMIT} heaviest",
                          drill_down_path(@repository, limit: nil, anchor: "spec-directory-durations"),
                          class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                 "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content" %>
            </p>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%# Directories exist and not one of their examples was timed. Every group's SUM is NULL,
              so there is a list of areas and no ranking — and a column of "not reported" under a
              heading promising "heaviest first" would be a ranking of nothing. Not zeroes, for the
              reason the panels above give: a zero is this surface inventing the measurement it is
              missing.

              Stated in DIRECTORIES, where the panel above states the same absence in files. Not a
              stylistic choice: `directory_count` is counted before the `LIMIT` and is exact,
              whereas summing `recorded_count` over the rows on hand would total a capped ten of
              them and print a suite-wide-looking figure covering part of the run. %>
          <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                title: "No timings on this run",
                description: "This run recorded examples in " \
                             "#{number_with_delimiter(@spec_directory_durations.directory_count)} " \
                             "#{'directory'.pluralize(@spec_directory_durations.directory_count)} " \
                             "and a duration for none of them, so there is no wall clock to " \
                             "attribute to any area. An example that never ran reports no duration."
              ) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
